echo '<div class="mk-prose">';
echo '<p class="mk-muted" style="margin-top:0;">An overview of biographical portraiture in Igbo and African history.</p>';
echo '<h2>The Problem of Individual Records</h2>';
echo '<p>Biographical history requires documents: letters, diaries, court records, newspaper accounts, photographs, testimony. For much of precolonial African history, these records were not produced in written form, or were not preserved, or were destroyed by colonial administrations who had little interest in African individual lives. The precolonial Igbo kept no written archives. What survives is oral tradition, archaeology, and the observations — often biased and incomplete — of European traders, missionaries, and administrators.</p>';
echo '<p>This means that biographical history of Igbo and African figures becomes possible, in detailed written form, primarily from the 18th century onward — with Equiano as the first major figure who left a substantial self-written record. Before him, individuals must be reconstructed from fragments.</p>';
echo '<h2>The Mission-School Generation</h2>';
echo '<p>The Church Missionary Society, the Roman Catholic Church, and the Methodist missions created the first generation of literate Igbo men and women who could document their own lives. Government College Umuahia, founded in 1929, educated a remarkable cohort — Achebe, Okigbo, Chukwuemeka Ike, Gabriel Okara — who went on to define Nigerian literature. Their autobiographies, interviews, and collected letters provide unusually rich biographical material for their generation.</p>';
echo '<h2>Women\'s Lives</h2>';
echo '<p>The biographical record for Igbo women is thinner and harder to recover. Most colonial archives recorded only men in positions of formal authority. Flora Nwapa\'s fiction, Buchi Emecheta\'s autobiographical novels, and a growing body of oral history research are gradually correcting this imbalance.</p>';
echo '<h2>The Biafra Generation</h2>';
echo '<p>The war of 1967–1970 produced an intense biographical record. International journalists documented Biafran leadership. Chinua Achebe, Wole Soyinka, and others wrote memoirs. The generation that survived the war and entered the diaspora has produced a substantial body of memoir, fiction, and testimony that constitutes one of the richest biographical archives in postcolonial African history.</p>';
